  "article": {
    "hero": {
      "title": "🗺️ Fictional Country Name Generator",
      "tagline": "Believable names for made-up modern countries, with an official title or a capital city."
    },
    "intro": "Thrillers, alternate histories and games often need a country that doesn't exist but sounds as if it might. This **fictional country name generator** makes believable modern countries: small European republics, Nordic states and island nations, with an official name like the Grand Duchy of Velaria, or a capital city.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Fictional Country or Fantasy Country?",
        "content": "<p>Use this generator for a country that could sit on a modern map: a small European republic in a spy thriller, an island nation in a satirical novel, or a delegation in a Model UN crisis. The names sound like real places and come with official titles and capitals.</p><p>For a kingdom in a world of dragons and castles, use the <a href=\"fantasy-country-name-generator.html\">Fantasy Country Name Generator</a> instead. Its names are made to sound older and stranger.</p><p>Writers have been inventing countries like this for a long time. Anthony Hope's <em>The Prisoner of Zenda</em> (1894) was set in Ruritania, which gave its name to a whole genre of stories set in small invented kingdoms.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "How real countries get their names",
